hy is the harmonic series a boundary case for $p$-series?","options":["It has $p=1$, exactly at the divergence threshold","It has $p=1$, the smallest exponent giving convergence","It has $p=-1$, because its terms are $n^{-1}$","It has $p=1$, where the terms do not tend to $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e30f10ba56a4ca79c053b114","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_series_0896d7ca_010_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $p<0$. What can be said about $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{p}}$?","options":["It converges, because a negative exponent makes the terms $\\frac{1}{n^{p}}$ small.","It is not a $p$-series, because $p$ must be positive.","Its behaviour is not decided, because the criterion $p>1$ only covers $p>0$.","It diverges, because its terms $n^{-p}$ do not tend to $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"64e41894f0b9f619ba2e2767","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_series_0896d7ca_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For which real values of $a$ does $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{a+1}}$ converge?","options":["$a>0$","$a\\ge0$","$a<0$","$a\\le1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4845b22d8358b4b3be3fb226","time_limit":30},{"id":"p_series_new_018_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which series is a convergent $p$-series?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{4/3}}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{1/2}}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1bd95dcea97c8da4a965236b","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_series_0896d7ca_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Consider the two series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}(0.9)^n$ and $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{0.9}}$. Which statement about their convergence is correct?","options":["Both diverge, because in each series the number $0.9$ is at most $1$.","$\\sum(0.9)^n$ converges and $\\sum n^{-0.9}$ diverges.","Both converge, because in each series the number $0.9$ is below $1$.","$\\sum(0.9)^n$ diverges and $\\sum n^{-0.9}$ converges."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9d3a33a24e9b85590151fa0","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_p_series_0896d7ca_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Does the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{1/2}+n^{3/2}}$ converge, and which argument shows it?","options":["It diverges: $n^{1/2}+n^{3/2}\\ge n^{1/2}$, so its terms are at most $\\frac{1}{n^{1/2}}$.","It converges: $n^{1/2}+n^{3/2}\\ge n^{2}$, so its terms are at most $\\frac{1}{n^{2}}$.","It converges: $n^{1/2}+n^{3/2}\\ge n^{3/2}$, so its terms are at most $\\frac{1}{n^{3/2}}$.","It is undecided, because its terms are not of the form $\\frac{1}{n^{p}}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"30be19bf2603e1673ccad014","time_limit":35}]}}